49c" :answer/id #uuid "8c0c36fa-3375-52d6-82c0-45e1fbe39799" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338818/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb92819-68b1-523c-9cee-c5ff8db19685" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-338818/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-338818/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-338818/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338818" :question/id #uuid "179d27a7-9c01-5e99-8f83-4c85bd8a80f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338818" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 4.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951126/assets/fd6dbbe8be077ed2eda955802f112540aa8aea73c5c65bae4f0f034176bbdccd.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338818/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "579373bc-38b3-584c-a118-84be7dfc9074"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338818"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338820/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "e92a8c48-b1af-5841-8c49-41167efc5675" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/e